# XBD 4.13 Host and Network Byte Orders
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The conversions are tested byte by byte in XSH.htonl.001 to .004. This
# module adds the use of the order in a real socket address: the port and
# address fields of a sockaddr_in hold their octets most significant first.

assertion  XBD.4.13-host-and-network-byte-orders.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.13
class      required
says       htons() and htonl() give values whose first octet in memory is
           the most significant (a port and 0x7f000001), ntohs() and
           ntohl() undo them, getsockname() reports a port bound with
           htons() with its octets in that order and the loopback address
           as 127, 0, 0, 1, and a connect() to a socket address whose port
           field was filled with the port's two octets, most significant
           first, reaches the listener bound with htons().
timeout    60
test       t/001-port-octets.c
